1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,214
Every reaction you'll ever
see in organic chemistry...

2
00:00:03,264 --> 00:00:06,960
is just electrons moving
from one place to another.

3
00:00:07,010 --> 00:00:12,763
That's it. So before anything else,
we need to answer two questions.

4
00:00:12,813 --> 00:00:14,370
Where are the electrons?

5
00:00:14,420 --> 00:00:16,200
And which ones actually matter?

6
00:00:16,700 --> 00:00:20,789
By the end of this video, you'll be able
to look at an atom, count the electrons

7
00:00:20,839 --> 00:00:23,697
that matter, and predict how
many bonds it's going to make.

8
00:00:23,747 --> 00:00:28,327
And you'll see why carbon, the star of
this whole course, shares its electrons

9
00:00:28,377 --> 00:00:29,647
instead of giving them away.

10
00:00:30,147 --> 00:00:34,397
Now, this topic usually gets rushed in
class, because it feels like review.

11
00:00:34,447 --> 00:00:35,686
But here's the thing.

12
00:00:35,736 --> 00:00:40,924
Almost every mistake later on, in Lewis
structures, in formal charge, even

13
00:00:40,974 --> 00:00:44,057
in mechanisms, comes back
to miscounting electrons.

14
00:00:44,107 --> 00:00:46,625
And the one idea that fixes it is this.

15
00:00:46,675 --> 00:00:49,121
Only the outer electrons do chemistry.

16
00:00:49,171 --> 00:00:51,667
Hold onto that. Let's build up to it.

17
00:00:54,717 --> 00:00:57,014
Okay, let's take a carbon atom apart.

18
00:00:57,064 --> 00:00:58,747
At the center is the nucleus.

19
00:00:58,797 --> 00:01:02,607
It's tiny, it's dense, and
it holds six protons...

20
00:01:02,657 --> 00:01:07,227
and six neutrons. Around
it move six electrons.

21
00:01:07,277 --> 00:01:10,437
And those three particles
build every atom there is.

22
00:01:10,937 --> 00:01:13,739
Each one has a charge and a mass.

23
00:01:13,789 --> 00:01:15,759
A proton is plus one.

24
00:01:15,809 --> 00:01:17,826
A neutron has no charge at all.

25
00:01:17,876 --> 00:01:20,159
It's neutral, that's
where the name comes from.

26
00:01:20,209 --> 00:01:22,439
And an electron is minus one.

27
00:01:22,489 --> 00:01:27,759
For mass, chemists use the atomic mass
unit, sized so a proton weighs about

28
00:01:27,809 --> 00:01:30,599
one. A neutron weighs about one too.

29
00:01:30,649 --> 00:01:34,172
And an electron? About
one eighteen hundredth.

30
00:01:34,222 --> 00:01:39,009
It's so light that an atom's mass is
basically just its protons and neutrons.

31
00:01:39,509 --> 00:01:42,699
Now here's the number that
matters most in this chapter.

32
00:01:42,749 --> 00:01:46,979
The number of protons is the
atomic number, written Z.

33
00:01:47,029 --> 00:01:48,816
And it's the atom's identity.

34
00:01:48,866 --> 00:01:55,459
Carbon has six. Every carbon atom,
everywhere, has exactly six protons.

35
00:01:55,509 --> 00:01:57,419
Add one more proton...

36
00:01:57,469 --> 00:01:58,812
and it's not carbon anymore.

37
00:01:58,862 --> 00:01:59,809
It's nitrogen.

38
00:02:00,309 --> 00:02:02,499
So think of the proton
count like an ID number.

39
00:02:02,549 --> 00:02:04,688
You can change an atom's charge.

40
00:02:04,738 --> 00:02:06,966
You can change how reactive it is.

41
00:02:07,016 --> 00:02:12,479
Organic chemistry does that constantly,
moving electrons on and off carbon.

42
00:02:12,529 --> 00:02:15,152
But none of that touches the protons...

43
00:02:15,202 --> 00:02:16,509
so carbon stays carbon.

44
00:02:19,559 --> 00:02:20,869
So what about neutrons?

45
00:02:20,919 --> 00:02:22,909
Most carbon atoms have six.

46
00:02:22,959 --> 00:02:25,077
But about one in a hundred has seven.

47
00:02:25,127 --> 00:02:28,656
Both are still carbon,
because both have six protons.

48
00:02:28,706 --> 00:02:32,085
Atoms of the same element with
different numbers of neutrons are called

49
00:02:32,135 --> 00:02:33,039
isotopes.

50
00:02:33,539 --> 00:02:36,721
To tell them apart, we
use the mass number, A.

51
00:02:36,771 --> 00:02:39,189
That's just protons plus neutrons.

52
00:02:39,239 --> 00:02:41,089
Six plus six, twelve.

53
00:02:41,139 --> 00:02:44,449
Carbon twelve. Six plus seven, thirteen.

54
00:02:44,499 --> 00:02:47,969
Carbon thirteen. Every sample
you'll handle has both.

55
00:02:48,019 --> 00:02:52,506
About ninety eight point nine percent
carbon twelve, and one point one percent

56
00:02:52,556 --> 00:02:53,659
carbon thirteen.

57
00:02:54,159 --> 00:02:55,589
And now notice something.

58
00:02:55,639 --> 00:02:58,489
Carbon thirteen is about
eight percent heavier.

59
00:02:58,539 --> 00:03:00,245
But look at the electrons.

60
00:03:00,295 --> 00:03:02,336
Same number, same arrangement.

61
00:03:02,386 --> 00:03:07,317
So the two form the same bonds,
at almost exactly the same rates.

62
00:03:07,367 --> 00:03:08,613
That's our first big clue.

63
00:03:08,663 --> 00:03:11,242
Bonding isn't about the neutrons.

64
00:03:11,292 --> 00:03:13,049
It's about the electrons.

65
00:03:13,099 --> 00:03:17,199
The protons only matter because they
set how many electrons there are.

66
00:03:17,699 --> 00:03:22,164
Quick check. An atom has seven
protons and eight neutrons.

67
00:03:22,214 --> 00:03:24,606
What element is it, and
what's its mass number?

68
00:03:29,106 --> 00:03:31,439
Seven protons makes it nitrogen.

69
00:03:31,489 --> 00:03:34,629
And seven plus eight gives
a mass number of fifteen.

70
00:03:34,679 --> 00:03:36,639
So it's nitrogen fifteen.

71
00:03:39,689 --> 00:03:41,239
Okay, so let's look at the electrons.

72
00:03:41,289 --> 00:03:43,879
Carbon's six electrons aren't all alike.

73
00:03:43,929 --> 00:03:45,979
Two sit close to the nucleus.

74
00:03:46,029 --> 00:03:48,039
The other four sit farther out.

75
00:03:48,089 --> 00:03:51,102
Chemists describe this with shells.

76
00:03:51,152 --> 00:03:54,769
Layers of electrons at increasing
distance from the nucleus.

77
00:03:55,269 --> 00:04:00,039
The innermost shell, called K,
holds at most two electrons.

78
00:04:00,089 --> 00:04:03,219
The next one, L, holds at most eight.

79
00:04:03,269 --> 00:04:07,759
Electrons fill the inner shell first,
because it's closer to the nucleus

80
00:04:07,809 --> 00:04:12,067
and lower in energy. So
carbon goes two in K...

81
00:04:12,117 --> 00:04:16,189
then four in L. We
write that as two, four.

82
00:04:16,689 --> 00:04:18,299
Now here's the key word.

83
00:04:18,349 --> 00:04:22,439
The electrons in the outermost
shell are the valence electrons.

84
00:04:22,489 --> 00:04:25,433
They're the only ones
that take part in bonding.

85
00:04:25,483 --> 00:04:31,212
Carbon has four. The inner ones are
called core electrons, and the nucleus

86
00:04:31,262 --> 00:04:34,572
holds them way too tightly
for them to do any chemistry.

87
00:04:34,622 --> 00:04:35,671
It's kind of like money.

88
00:04:35,721 --> 00:04:38,569
Core electrons are locked
in a savings account.

89
00:04:38,619 --> 00:04:41,239
Valence electrons are
the cash in your pocket.

90
00:04:41,289 --> 00:04:43,809
They're the only ones you actually spend.

91
00:04:44,309 --> 00:04:45,659
Let's try one together.

92
00:04:45,709 --> 00:04:51,779
Nitrogen. Z is seven, so a neutral
nitrogen atom has seven electrons.

93
00:04:51,829 --> 00:04:53,479
Two fill the K shell.

94
00:04:53,529 --> 00:04:55,346
The other five go into L.

95
00:04:55,396 --> 00:04:58,559
So nitrogen has five valence electrons.

96
00:04:58,609 --> 00:05:01,372
And the L shell has room for eight...

97
00:05:01,422 --> 00:05:04,546
so it's three short. Hold
onto that number three.

98
00:05:04,596 --> 00:05:05,969
It's about to predict something.

99
00:05:06,469 --> 00:05:09,559
But you don't have to fill
shells by hand every time.

100
00:05:09,609 --> 00:05:11,579
The periodic table does it for you.

101
00:05:11,629 --> 00:05:13,909
Its columns are called groups.

102
00:05:13,959 --> 00:05:18,199
And in the main groups, every element
in a column has the same number of

103
00:05:18,249 --> 00:05:21,559
valence electrons. Helium
is the one exception.

104
00:05:21,609 --> 00:05:24,626
In groups one and two, the
group number is the count.

105
00:05:24,676 --> 00:05:29,673
Hydrogen has one. In groups
thirteen to eighteen, subtract ten.

106
00:05:29,723 --> 00:05:33,623
Carbon is in group fourteen,
so fourteen minus ten...

107
00:05:33,673 --> 00:05:38,239
four. That ten comes from the ten
transition metal columns in the middle,

108
00:05:38,289 --> 00:05:40,689
which organic chemistry rarely needs.

109
00:05:41,189 --> 00:05:44,152
So here are the numbers
you'll use all course long.

110
00:05:44,202 --> 00:05:47,959
Hydrogen, one. Carbon, four.

111
00:05:48,009 --> 00:05:51,839
Nitrogen, five. Oxygen, six.

112
00:05:51,889 --> 00:05:56,886
And the halogens, fluorine,
chlorine, bromine and iodine...

113
00:05:56,936 --> 00:05:57,736
seven.

114
00:05:57,889 --> 00:06:01,649
Quick check. How many valence
electrons does oxygen have?

115
00:06:05,649 --> 00:06:07,665
Oxygen is in group sixteen.

116
00:06:07,715 --> 00:06:11,525
Sixteen minus ten...
six valence electrons.

117
00:06:11,575 --> 00:06:15,735
Not eight. Eight is its total,
and two of those are core.

118
00:06:18,785 --> 00:06:21,391
So why do valence
electrons matter so much?

119
00:06:21,441 --> 00:06:24,955
Look at neon. Its electrons go two, eight.

120
00:06:25,005 --> 00:06:27,695
The outer shell is completely full.

121
00:06:27,745 --> 00:06:31,082
And neon forms no compounds
under ordinary conditions.

122
00:06:31,132 --> 00:06:33,375
It just... doesn't react.

123
00:06:33,425 --> 00:06:37,145
Helium, with its one shell
full at two, is the same.

124
00:06:37,645 --> 00:06:40,888
Now remember nitrogen,
three electrons short?

125
00:06:40,938 --> 00:06:46,403
In ammonia, nitrogen shares one pair of
electrons with each of three hydrogens.

126
00:06:46,453 --> 00:06:50,875
Each shared pair has one electron
from nitrogen and one from hydrogen.

127
00:06:50,925 --> 00:06:51,875
And here's the trick.

128
00:06:51,925 --> 00:06:54,531
Both atoms count the whole pair.

129
00:06:54,581 --> 00:06:57,435
Each shared pair is one bond.

130
00:06:57,485 --> 00:07:01,187
Nitrogen also keeps one pair to
itself that it doesn't share.

131
00:07:01,237 --> 00:07:03,165
That's called a lone pair.

132
00:07:03,665 --> 00:07:05,475
Now count around nitrogen.

133
00:07:05,525 --> 00:07:09,543
Two, four, six from the shared pairs...

134
00:07:09,593 --> 00:07:11,388
plus two from the lone pair.

135
00:07:11,438 --> 00:07:15,555
Eight. And each hydrogen
counts its one shared pair.

136
00:07:15,605 --> 00:07:18,465
Two electrons, which fills its only shell.

137
00:07:18,965 --> 00:07:23,208
Carbon in methane does the same
thing with four shared pairs.

138
00:07:23,258 --> 00:07:26,975
Eight around carbon. Two
around each hydrogen.

139
00:07:27,025 --> 00:07:29,315
And that's the octet rule.

140
00:07:29,365 --> 00:07:34,155
An atom is most stable, lowest in
energy, with a full outer shell.

141
00:07:34,205 --> 00:07:39,375
For carbon, nitrogen, oxygen and
the halogens, full means eight.

142
00:07:39,425 --> 00:07:42,445
For hydrogen, full means two.

143
00:07:42,945 --> 00:07:44,695
And now the counting pays off.

144
00:07:44,745 --> 00:07:48,948
Carbon has four, needs eight,
so it forms four bonds.

145
00:07:48,998 --> 00:07:52,162
Nitrogen has five, so three bonds.

146
00:07:52,212 --> 00:07:55,042
Oxygen has six, two bonds.

147
00:07:55,092 --> 00:07:58,755
A halogen has seven, one bond.

148
00:07:58,805 --> 00:08:02,999
And hydrogen just needs one
more to reach two, so one bond.

149
00:08:03,049 --> 00:08:06,855
Four, three, two, one, one.

150
00:08:06,905 --> 00:08:10,785
You'll see this pattern in almost
every neutral molecule from here on.

151
00:08:11,285 --> 00:08:12,895
One common slip, though.

152
00:08:12,945 --> 00:08:16,451
The octet rule counts
electrons, not neighbors.

153
00:08:16,501 --> 00:08:20,075
Look at ethene. Each carbon
only has three neighbors.

154
00:08:20,125 --> 00:08:22,195
Two hydrogens and the other carbon.

155
00:08:22,245 --> 00:08:24,795
But the two carbons share two pairs.

156
00:08:24,845 --> 00:08:29,155
A double bond. Count the electrons,
and each carbon still has eight.

157
00:08:29,205 --> 00:08:32,975
So if you count neighbors instead, the
first double bond you meet will fool

158
00:08:33,025 --> 00:08:33,805
you.

159
00:08:33,825 --> 00:08:38,131
And if you ever see a carbon with
only three bonds and nothing else...

160
00:08:38,181 --> 00:08:42,168
that's not a typo. That atom is the
interesting part of the molecule, and

161
00:08:42,218 --> 00:08:43,715
it usually carries a charge.

162
00:08:43,765 --> 00:08:47,288
The other possibility, called a
radical, comes later in the course.

163
00:08:47,338 --> 00:08:49,935
We'll see the charged case
at the end of this video.

164
00:08:49,985 --> 00:08:53,165
But first, we need to know
what charge even means here.

165
00:08:56,215 --> 00:09:01,429
Take sodium. Z is eleven, so its
electrons go two, eight, one.

166
00:09:01,479 --> 00:09:06,325
That single outer electron is far
from the nucleus and loosely held.

167
00:09:06,375 --> 00:09:08,112
If sodium loses it...

168
00:09:08,162 --> 00:09:10,045
it's left with two, eight.

169
00:09:10,095 --> 00:09:14,415
A full outer shell. That's why sodium
gives that electron up so easily.

170
00:09:14,915 --> 00:09:16,585
But now count the charge.

171
00:09:16,635 --> 00:09:18,538
Still eleven protons.

172
00:09:18,588 --> 00:09:21,608
Those never changed.
But only ten electrons.

173
00:09:21,658 --> 00:09:24,412
Eleven minus ten is plus one.

174
00:09:24,462 --> 00:09:28,945
An atom with more protons than
electrons is positive, and it's called a

175
00:09:28,995 --> 00:09:31,305
cation. Here's a memory trick.

176
00:09:31,355 --> 00:09:33,995
A cation is pawsitive.

177
00:09:34,495 --> 00:09:36,458
Fluorine goes the other way.

178
00:09:36,508 --> 00:09:39,738
It's two, seven. One electron short.

179
00:09:39,788 --> 00:09:42,709
So it gains one, and reaches two, eight.

180
00:09:42,759 --> 00:09:47,578
Nine protons, ten electrons,
so the charge is minus one.

181
00:09:47,628 --> 00:09:52,655
An atom with more electrons than protons
is negative, and that's an anion.

182
00:09:53,155 --> 00:09:55,375
So here's the rule for any ion.

183
00:09:55,425 --> 00:09:58,858
Charge equals protons minus electrons.

184
00:09:58,908 --> 00:10:00,790
The nucleus never changes.

185
00:10:00,840 --> 00:10:02,608
Only the electrons move.

186
00:10:05,658 --> 00:10:07,868
So there are two ways to get a full shell.

187
00:10:07,918 --> 00:10:12,028
Put sodium next to chlorine,
which is two, eight, seven.

188
00:10:12,078 --> 00:10:16,598
One short. Sodium's outer electron
moves over to chlorine completely.

189
00:10:16,648 --> 00:10:21,592
Now we have a sodium ion and a chloride
ion, and opposite charges attract.

190
00:10:21,642 --> 00:10:24,093
That attraction is an ionic bond.

191
00:10:24,143 --> 00:10:25,138
Nothing is shared.

192
00:10:25,638 --> 00:10:27,876
Now take two hydrogen atoms.

193
00:10:27,926 --> 00:10:30,648
Each has one electron and wants two.

194
00:10:30,698 --> 00:10:32,921
Neither will hand its electron away.

195
00:10:32,971 --> 00:10:37,892
So they share. The two electrons sit
between the nuclei as one pair, and

196
00:10:37,942 --> 00:10:40,028
each atom counts both.

197
00:10:40,078 --> 00:10:44,078
A shared pair holding two atoms
together is a covalent bond.

198
00:10:44,578 --> 00:10:47,008
So which way does an atom go?

199
00:10:47,058 --> 00:10:51,653
An ionic bond needs a giver, like
sodium, with an electron or two past a

200
00:10:51,703 --> 00:10:56,921
full shell. And a taker, like
chlorine, an electron or two short.

201
00:10:56,971 --> 00:10:58,528
But now think about carbon.

202
00:10:58,578 --> 00:11:01,061
It has four, and it needs eight.

203
00:11:01,111 --> 00:11:02,351
It's right in the middle.

204
00:11:02,851 --> 00:11:05,969
To give away four, it would become C4+.

205
00:11:06,019 --> 00:11:11,018
Pulling four electrons off a small
atom costs a huge amount of energy.

206
00:11:11,068 --> 00:11:14,225
Taking on four makes C4−.

207
00:11:14,275 --> 00:11:17,908
Four extra negative
charges on one small atom.

208
00:11:17,958 --> 00:11:22,308
Just as costly. So carbon
does the smart thing.

209
00:11:22,358 --> 00:11:28,521
It shares. Each shared pair counts for
both atoms, so everyone gets to eight,

210
00:11:28,571 --> 00:11:30,411
and nobody takes on a full charge.

211
00:11:30,911 --> 00:11:35,674
And nitrogen, oxygen and the halogens
hold their own electrons tightly,

212
00:11:35,724 --> 00:11:36,961
so they're not givers.

213
00:11:37,011 --> 00:11:40,768
And carbon and hydrogen don't
hand their electrons over either.

214
00:11:40,818 --> 00:11:44,928
So when these atoms bond to each
other, there's no giver in the pair.

215
00:11:44,978 --> 00:11:49,328
So they share too. And that's why
organic molecules are held together by

216
00:11:49,378 --> 00:11:50,671
covalent bonds.

217
00:11:51,171 --> 00:11:56,490
One more thing. When two different
atoms share a pair, one usually pulls

218
00:11:56,540 --> 00:11:59,445
harder, and the pair
sits closer to that atom.

219
00:11:59,495 --> 00:12:02,261
That's a polar covalent bond.

220
00:12:02,311 --> 00:12:04,128
Shared, but unequally.

221
00:12:04,178 --> 00:12:09,301
Bonds from carbon to oxygen, nitrogen,
fluorine or chlorine are clearly

222
00:12:09,351 --> 00:12:14,074
polar. Carbon to carbon and carbon
to hydrogen are close to nonpolar.

223
00:12:14,124 --> 00:12:17,151
We'll put numbers on that in
the electronegativity video.

224
00:12:20,201 --> 00:12:23,551
Okay. Now let's go back to that
carbon with only three bonds.

225
00:12:23,601 --> 00:12:28,231
Start with a carbon bonded to three
carbon groups, which we write as R,

226
00:12:28,281 --> 00:12:33,324
and a chlorine. Now say the carbon
chlorine bond breaks, and the chlorine

227
00:12:33,374 --> 00:12:35,978
takes both electrons of that pair with it.

228
00:12:36,028 --> 00:12:39,731
Chlorine leaves as chloride, minus one.

229
00:12:39,781 --> 00:12:43,751
And carbon is left with three
bonds and a positive charge.

230
00:12:43,801 --> 00:12:45,681
That's a carbocation.

231
00:12:46,181 --> 00:12:47,464
Here's where it gets interesting.

232
00:12:47,514 --> 00:12:53,078
There are two different ways to count
carbon's electrons, and both are right.

233
00:12:53,128 --> 00:12:56,691
First, count around carbon, for the octet.

234
00:12:56,741 --> 00:12:59,104
Before, it had four pairs.

235
00:12:59,154 --> 00:13:03,011
Eight electrons. Now it has three pairs.

236
00:13:03,061 --> 00:13:07,961
Just six. So it's two short of an
octet, and that leaves it with an empty

237
00:13:08,011 --> 00:13:11,981
orbital. Room for a pair,
but no electrons in it.

238
00:13:12,481 --> 00:13:17,117
Second, count carbon's own
electrons, for the charge.

239
00:13:17,167 --> 00:13:21,986
In a shared pair, one
electron belongs to each atom.

240
00:13:22,036 --> 00:13:25,977
Before, carbon owned one
from each of four bonds.

241
00:13:26,027 --> 00:13:28,824
Four. The same as a neutral carbon atom.

242
00:13:28,874 --> 00:13:33,937
Now it owns three. One short
of neutral, so plus one.

243
00:13:33,987 --> 00:13:39,933
And that gap, six instead of eight,
is exactly why a carbocation reacts

244
00:13:39,983 --> 00:13:43,104
so readily with anything
that can give it a pair.

245
00:13:43,154 --> 00:13:46,647
You'll meet these again
in SN1 and E1 reactions.

246
00:13:47,147 --> 00:13:52,293
Now flip it around. Say a strong
base pulls off a hydrogen as H+.

247
00:13:52,343 --> 00:13:55,069
A bare proton, no electrons.

248
00:13:55,119 --> 00:13:58,997
The pair stays behind on
carbon as a lone pair.

249
00:13:59,047 --> 00:14:03,890
Count around carbon. Three
bonds plus a lone pair.

250
00:14:03,940 --> 00:14:06,904
Still eight. Count what it owns.

251
00:14:06,954 --> 00:14:10,217
Three, plus two... five.

252
00:14:10,267 --> 00:14:13,301
One more than neutral, so minus one.

253
00:14:13,351 --> 00:14:19,087
That's a carbanion. A full
octet, plus a spare pair to give.

254
00:14:22,137 --> 00:14:23,687
So let's put it all in one picture.

255
00:14:23,737 --> 00:14:25,827
Protons set the element.

256
00:14:25,877 --> 00:14:29,687
Valence electrons, straight from
the group number, set the chemistry.

257
00:14:29,737 --> 00:14:32,634
Atoms want a full outer shell.

258
00:14:32,684 --> 00:14:36,367
Eight for carbon, nitrogen,
oxygen and the halogens.

259
00:14:36,417 --> 00:14:42,987
Two for hydrogen. So carbon makes four
bonds, nitrogen three, oxygen two,

260
00:14:43,037 --> 00:14:45,424
and hydrogen and the halogens one.

261
00:14:45,474 --> 00:14:47,847
Carbon doesn't give or take.

262
00:14:47,897 --> 00:14:52,430
It shares. And when a carbon has
only three bonds, count twice.

263
00:14:52,480 --> 00:14:54,547
Around it, for the octet.

264
00:14:54,597 --> 00:14:57,057
And what it owns, for the charge.

265
00:15:00,107 --> 00:15:01,084
Alright, practice time.

266
00:15:01,134 --> 00:15:03,967
Pause the video, try each one,
then I'll walk you through it.

267
00:15:04,017 --> 00:15:07,517
Problem one. Chlorine
is in group seventeen.

268
00:15:07,567 --> 00:15:11,497
How many valence electrons does it
have, and how many bonds does it usually

269
00:15:11,547 --> 00:15:12,347
form?

270
00:15:16,487 --> 00:15:18,730
Group seventeen, so subtract ten.

271
00:15:18,780 --> 00:15:20,717
Seven valence electrons.

272
00:15:20,767 --> 00:15:25,637
Seven needs one more to reach eight,
so chlorine forms one bond, like in

273
00:15:25,687 --> 00:15:27,869
HCl. And the trap answer
here is seventeen.

274
00:15:27,919 --> 00:15:31,603
That's the group number,
not the valence count.

275
00:15:31,653 --> 00:15:32,847
Always subtract the ten.

276
00:15:33,347 --> 00:15:38,133
Problem two. A particle has
eight protons and ten electrons.

277
00:15:38,183 --> 00:15:42,687
What element is it, what's its charge,
and is it a cation or an anion?

278
00:15:47,187 --> 00:15:49,517
Eight protons, so it's oxygen.

279
00:15:49,567 --> 00:15:52,811
The protons decide the
element, not the electrons.

280
00:15:52,861 --> 00:15:55,897
Charge is protons minus electrons.

281
00:15:55,947 --> 00:15:58,553
Eight minus ten... minus two.

282
00:15:58,603 --> 00:16:01,737
More electrons than
protons, so it's an anion.

283
00:16:01,787 --> 00:16:04,160
The oxide ion. The trap?

284
00:16:04,210 --> 00:16:07,033
Seeing ten electrons and saying neon.

285
00:16:07,083 --> 00:16:09,033
Neon has ten protons.

286
00:16:09,083 --> 00:16:10,227
This has eight.

287
00:16:10,727 --> 00:16:15,045
Last one. A carbon has three
bonds and one lone pair.

288
00:16:15,095 --> 00:16:18,698
How many electrons are around it,
what's its charge, and what do we call

289
00:16:18,748 --> 00:16:19,548
it?

290
00:16:23,475 --> 00:16:25,005
Count around it first.

291
00:16:25,055 --> 00:16:28,118
Three bonds is six, plus
a lone pair is eight.

292
00:16:28,168 --> 00:16:31,058
A full octet. Now count what it owns.

293
00:16:31,108 --> 00:16:35,462
One from each bond, that's three,
plus both lone pair electrons, two.

294
00:16:35,512 --> 00:16:39,771
Five. A neutral carbon owns
four, so this one is minus one.

295
00:16:39,821 --> 00:16:45,278
It's a carbanion. The trap is seeing
three bonds and saying carbocation.

296
00:16:45,328 --> 00:16:48,918
A carbocation has three
bonds and no lone pair.

297
00:16:48,968 --> 00:16:50,928
So always check for that lone pair.

298
00:16:53,978 --> 00:16:56,568
And that's atomic structure
for organic chemistry.

299
00:16:56,618 --> 00:17:00,608
If you want more practice on this, there
are free questions on levlprep.com.

300
00:17:00,658 --> 00:17:07,475
Next up, orbitals. Where these valence
electrons actually are, and why that

301
00:17:07,525 --> 00:17:09,568
gives molecules their shapes.

302
00:17:09,618 --> 00:17:10,418
I'll see you there.
